## Burnout Prevention at the Spa — When Body and Mind Say Enough

Burnout does not arrive all at once. It creeps in over months of overtime, unanswered messages to friends and evenings spent over a laptop. First the joy of work disappears, then the energy for anything else, and finally that strange state sets in where you feel tired even after a whole weekend in bed. Burnout prevention at the spa is not a fashionable trend — it is a return to a principle that has held in Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) for two centuries: giving body and mind room to recover in an environment that was quite literally created for rest.

In the Czech Republic, burnout is still spoken of in a whisper — as if admitting exhaustion were the same as admitting weakness. It is not. And the spa environment offers a way to stop with dignity and under medical supervision.

## How to Recognise That Burnout Is Knocking at the Door

Burnout is not just tiredness from work. In 2019 the World Health Organization described it in the International Classification of Diseases (ICD‑11) as an occupational phenomenon — the result of chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. It is not a separate disease diagnosis but a state that shows itself on three levels:

**The physical level** — persistent fatigue that does not lift even after a holiday. Headaches, tension in the shoulders and neck, sleep disturbances. Many people begin to go off sick more often without any specific diagnosis.

**The emotional level** — a feeling of emptiness, cynicism, loss of empathy. Things that used to bring pleasure stop evoking any emotion at all. You function on autopilot.

**The cognitive level** — difficulty concentrating, forgetfulness, an inability to make decisions.

If you recognise yourself in this description, you are not alone. One thing, however, needs saying right at the start: a spa stay does not replace psychotherapy, psychiatric care or a change in working conditions. If the exhaustion has lasted for months or is accompanied by anxiety or depression, first see your general practitioner, a psychologist or a psychiatrist. The spa is then a complement, not an alternative.

## Why the Spa Environment Helps against Stress

Rest at home is often not enough, because you remain in the midst of what triggers the stress. A spa stay changes three things at once: the environment, the rhythm and the amount of movement.

### Disconnecting from Stress Triggers

Burnout is tied to context — to the office, to the sound of notifications, to certain people and situations. A single day off at home does not change that context. A spa stay means physical distance from everything that triggers stress. Mariánské Lázně is two hours from Prague, but atmospherically on another planet. There is mobile signal here, but between the forests and the colonnade you will find yourself reaching for your phone less and less.

### A Return to a Regular Rhythm

The spa regimen has its order — the morning drinking cure, treatments before noon, an afternoon walk, a quiet evening. A steady daily rhythm with exercise outdoors and regular sleep is precisely what an overworked person usually lacks. Many guests describe how, during the stay, they begin to wake without an alarm and feel hunger at regular times again.

### The Forest within Reach

The Slavkov Forest surrounding Mariánské Lázně is not just a backdrop for walks. A meta-analysis of eight studies found lower salivary cortisol after time in the forest than in the city; it is a short-term effect and the authors point out that expectation may play a role ([Antonelli et al., 2019](https://consensus.app/papers/details/9e2f3fee63dc533fa75d4391954c57d4/), *Int J Biometeorol*). A meta-analysis of twenty studies described a short-term reduction in anxiety after time in the forest; long-term follow-up is lacking ([Kotera et al., 2020](https://consensus.app/papers/details/04a95ffe5fbf5763964d7a07a31a85f8/), *Int J Ment Health Addict*). Marked trails lead from every spa hotel straight into the forest — more on this in the article on [forest bathing in the Slavkov Forest](/en/magazine/forest-bathing-mindfulness-slavkov).

## Treatments That Belong to a Stay against Stress

Which treatments you receive is decided by the spa physician at the initial examination according to your state of health. The following selection is the one physicians most often include for guests with a high degree of stress.

### Mineral Baths

Mariánské Lázně has more than [40 mineral springs](/en/mineral-springs) with different chemical compositions. A bath in warm natural mineral water lasts around twenty minutes and is one of the traditional spa treatments used to ease muscle tension and calm the whole body. It was baths that formed the basis of the programmes in the studies we describe below.

### Dry Carbonic Baths (CO₂ Therapy)

A treatment in which you lie in a sealed bag filled with natural carbon dioxide from the Maria Spring. According to a review of the specialist literature, a carbonic bath increases skin blood flow and produces a sensation of warmth; the review's authors at the same time call for methodologically better studies ([Pagourelias et al., 2011](https://consensus.app/papers/details/cffd890e39e252b5bf9e66f5a36c8ff6/), *Int J Biometeorol*). Guests describe it as one of the most pleasant treatments of all. Details can be found in the article on [CO₂ therapy](/en/magazine/co2-gas-injections).

### Massage and Manual Therapy

Chronic stress is stored in the body — literally. Trapezius muscles as hard as stone, shortened flexors from whole days at the computer, a painful lower back. Massage in the spa setting is not just about relaxing music and fragrant oils. It is targeted work with the soft tissues, which the physiotherapist adapts to where it hurts. The physician often combines classic massage with peat wraps.

### The Terrain Cure

Mariánské Lázně lies at an altitude of around 630 metres, surrounded by coniferous forests. Terrain therapy — the Terrainkur — has been a tradition here since the 19th century: the physician prescribes regular walks along marked routes with gradients matched to your fitness. For someone who sits all day, it is often the first regular exercise in years. More on the page about [climate therapy](/en/climate-therapy).

## What the Research Says about Spa Stays and Burnout

Controlled studies exist above all in people with a high degree of work-related stress — that is, precisely the group at risk of burnout.

The most convincing is a German randomised trial with 109 working people with above-average stress: a three-week programme at a spa resort combining mud treatments, relaxation, exercise and stress-management training reduced perceived stress and emotional exhaustion, and the difference from the waiting-list group lasted six months ([Stier‑Jarmer et al., 2016](https://doi.org/10.3238/arztebl.2016.0781), *Dtsch Arztebl Int*). The programme also included a psychological intervention, so the effect cannot be attributed to the treatments alone — but it is precisely the combination that the spa offers.

In an Austrian pilot study with 65 working people with burnout symptoms, fatigue, tension, motivation and sleep improved after three weeks of spa treatment, and the improvement lasted three months after returning home ([Blasche et al., 2010](https://doi.org/10.1159/000315301), *Forsch Komplementmed*). The study had no control group.

That shorter stays work too is shown by two Lithuanian randomised trials. In 243 people with moderate to high stress, eleven days of balneotherapy reduced stress, anxiety, depressive symptoms and sleep disturbances, and part of the effect lasted six months ([Rapolienė et al., 2026](https://doi.org/10.1186/s12906-026-05323-4), *BMC Complement Med Ther*). In 373 people with stress, programmes of 6–11 days improved fatigue, sleep and quality of life, likewise for up to half a year ([Rapolienė et al., 2025](https://consensus.app/papers/details/9b8af91815645dce946c8b1e5c4f8834/), *Int J Biometeorol*). Both studies rest on the participants' self-assessment. Five months of regular bathing in 362 "sub-healthy" adults in China then led to fewer sleep disturbances and less psychological stress than in the control group ([Yang et al., 2018](https://consensus.app/papers/details/5da141d1b4205f63a3d401732e517a95/), *Int J Biometeorol*); the study was not randomised.

And cortisol, the "stress hormone"? A meta-analysis of 17 studies with 765 participants found a short-term drop in cortisol after balneotherapy in healthy people with high stress; the pooled difference between treated and control groups, however, did not reach statistical significance ([Antonelli et al., 2024](https://consensus.app/papers/details/b207a63a0d4850fd9314b16f22fc3dc3/), *Int J Biometeorol*). More practical than laboratory values, then, are the results you feel: less fatigue, better sleep, more energy.

## A Practical Guide: How to Plan Your Stay

### Length of Stay

A stay against stress and burnout is preventive; you come as a self-paying guest and choose the length in consultation with the spa physician. In the studies above, both programmes of around eleven days and the traditional three-week cure worked; a shorter wellness weekend is regenerative rather than therapeutic in character. Details of the types of stay can be found in the [spa treatment guide](/en/magazine/spa-treatment-guide).

### What to Expect

The first days are, paradoxically, the hardest. A body used to constant rush reacts to calm with restlessness — the urge to check e‑mails, trouble falling asleep. This is common and usually passes within a few days. Many guests then describe a feeling they sum up in the words "I finally feel like myself again".

### Insurance Coverage

Burnout syndrome is not listed in the Czech [indication list](/en/indications-and-contraindications), so covered spa care cannot be obtained for it. Its accompanying conditions, however, can be indications — for example hypertension of grade II to III (indication II/3, comprehensive care 28 days or contributory care 21 days) or painful syndromes of the spine (group VII). For guests insured in the Czech Republic, a general practitioner can issue a referral for spa care if they identify a specific diagnosis; in contributory care the insurer covers treatment, while accommodation and meals are paid by the guest. A self-paid stay is a further option — details can be found in the [spa treatment guide](/en/magazine/spa-treatment-guide).

### When a Stay Is Not Suitable

Acute infection, fever and diseases in the acute stage are among the general [contraindications to spa care](/en/indications-and-contraindications). If you are being treated for a chronic condition or take psychiatric medication, consult your doctor before booking.

### What to Pack

Leave the laptop at home. Seriously. Take the book you have been meaning to read, comfortable walking shoes and a swimsuit. A notebook for the thoughts that come when the carousel finally stops. You can buy a spa cup on the spot — it belongs to the ritual of the morning drinking cure on the colonnade.

## Why Mariánské Lázně

There are dozens of spa towns in the Czech Republic. Mariánské Lázně has several specific advantages for a stay against stress.

**A two-hundred-year tradition** — the town was founded in 1818 as a place for healing and recovery. It is not a converted holiday resort; it is a town whose entire urban structure serves rest. Wide promenades, extensive parks, architecture designed to calm.

**Natural healing resources** — over 40 mineral springs and natural CO₂ from the Maria Spring. What rises from the ground here has been used in spa medicine for generations.

**The forest right behind the hotel** — the Slavkov Forest is one of the best-preserved forest areas in the Czech Republic. Clean air, silence, minimal light pollution. And as the studies above show, time in the forest is one of the best-documented ways of reducing stress in the short term.

**Accessibility** — two hours by car from Prague, one hour from Plzeň, two hours from Nuremberg. No need to fly, no need for complicated planning. The Ensana hotels in Mariánské Lázně offer [stay programmes](https://ensanahotels.com/en/offers) that include an initial examination, an individual treatment plan and meals — just arrive and let yourself be guided.

## Burnout Is Not Failure

Czech working culture tends to glorify overwork. Someone who leaves the office on time arouses suspicion. Anyone who takes time off "just because" is not ambitious enough. This attitude is a reliable recipe for burnout — and changing it does not begin in the office, but with admitting to yourself that recovery is not a luxury but a necessity.

A spa stay in Mariánské Lázně is one way to set that recovery in motion. Not because it is a magical place, but because for two hundred years people here have been perfecting the art of helping others slow down, take a breath and return to life with more energy. If what exhausts you does not change too after you return, the fatigue will come back — and that is exactly why the stay is a beginning, not a solution. But a beginning which, according to the studies, lasts several months.
